            <ArticleTitle>Driving Rel-iant Tregs toward an Identity Crisis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>391-393</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1074-7613(17)30372-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.immuni.2017.08.014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Inhibiting Treg cell function in tumors is an attractive strategy to improve anti-cancer immunity. In a pair of papers in Immunity and Cell, Ghosh and colleagues show that the canonical NF-κB subunits p65 and c-Rel have non-redundant, critical roles in promoting Treg cell development and function (Oh et al., 2017). Targeting c-Rel blunts Treg cell immunosuppressive activity in the tumor microenvironment and enhances anti-tumor T cell responses (Grinberg-Bleyer et al., 2017).</AbstractText>
